MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "A Fool To Care feels like a natural follow-up to Memphis because I want more of Boz Scaggs's soulful sound.",
    "The Breeze connects directly to my interest in Old Sock: more Clapton with relaxed blues guitar is especially appealing today. Cream and Super Session also fit that blues-oriented interest."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "In-A-Gadda-Da-Vida shares a connection to older guitar rock, but its heavy, extended psychedelia misses the relaxed blues and soul I want now.",
    "The stripped-down live songwriting of Live at The Old Quarter and the instrumental fusion of Enigmatic Ocean lack the vocal blues-and-roots feel I am seeking; the Bowie collection also feels outside that mood."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "My interest in Clapton and Hendrix seems to have been stretched into a broader interest in classic rock, although the bluesier side is what appeals to me today.",
    "Roots-related music seems to have been treated as interchangeable, but a sparse live singer-songwriter performance does not satisfy the same desire as a soulful band album."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "The direct connections to Memphis and Old Sock matter more to my choices today than a broad connection to the same musical era.",
    "An album can fit my taste without being something I would choose now: I would save Cream, Super Session, and Joe Cocker for later while choosing a newer blues-and-soul album today."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "The recommendations understood my interest in blues and roots music, but sometimes stretched it beyond the relaxed, soulful vocal albums I want today."
}
